# Proof

**Claim:** $x_t \to z_*$ as $t \to \infty$.

**Setup.** Define the error $e_t := x_t - z_*$. Since $z_*$ is the fixed point of $T$, we have $Tz_* = z_*$. Using the update rule
$$x_t = (1-\gamma_t)x_{t-1} + \gamma_t\, Tx_{t-1},$$
we compute
$$e_t = (1-\gamma_t)(x_{t-1}-z_*) + \gamma_t(Tx_{t-1}-z_*) = (1-\gamma_t)\,e_{t-1} + \gamma_t\,(Tx_{t-1}-Tz_*).$$

**Error recursion.** Since $T$ is $\alpha$-Lipschitz with $\alpha < 1$,
$$\|Tx_{t-1}-Tz_*\| \le \alpha\,\|x_{t-1}-z_*\| = \alpha\,\|e_{t-1}\|.$$
Taking norms and applying the triangle inequality:
$$\|e_t\| \le (1-\gamma_t)\|e_{t-1}\| + \gamma_t\,\|Tx_{t-1}-Tz_*\| \le (1-\gamma_t)\|e_{t-1}\| + \gamma_t\,\alpha\,\|e_{t-1}\|.$$
Therefore
$$\|e_t\| \le \bigl(1 - \gamma_t(1-\alpha)\bigr)\,\|e_{t-1}\|.$$

**Key parameter.** Set $\beta_t := \gamma_t(1-\alpha)$. Since $0 < \gamma_t < 1$ and $0 \le \alpha < 1$, we have
$$0 < \beta_t < 1-\alpha \le 1,$$
so $1 - \beta_t \in (0,1)$ for every $t$. The recursion becomes
$$\|e_t\| \le (1-\beta_t)\,\|e_{t-1}\|.$$

**Iterating.** By induction on $t$,
$$\|e_t\| \le \|e_0\|\,\prod_{s=1}^{t}(1-\beta_s).$$

**The product vanishes.** Since $0 < \beta_s < 1$, we use the standard inequality $\log(1-u) \le -u$ for $u \in [0,1)$:
$$\log\!\left(\prod_{s=1}^{t}(1-\beta_s)\right) = \sum_{s=1}^{t}\log(1-\beta_s) \le -\sum_{s=1}^{t}\beta_s = -(1-\alpha)\sum_{s=1}^{t}\gamma_s.$$

By hypothesis $\sum_{t=1}^{\infty}\gamma_t = \infty$, and $1-\alpha > 0$, hence
$$\sum_{s=1}^{t}\beta_s = (1-\alpha)\sum_{s=1}^{t}\gamma_s \;\longrightarrow\; +\infty.$$

Therefore
$$\log\!\left(\prod_{s=1}^{t}(1-\beta_s)\right) \le -(1-\alpha)\sum_{s=1}^{t}\gamma_s \;\longrightarrow\; -\infty,$$
which gives
$$\prod_{s=1}^{t}(1-\beta_s) \;\longrightarrow\; 0.$$

**Conclusion.** Since $\|e_0\|$ is a fixed finite constant and $\prod_{s=1}^{t}(1-\beta_s) \to 0$, we obtain
$$\|x_t - z_*\| = \|e_t\| \le \|e_0\|\prod_{s=1}^{t}(1-\beta_s) \;\longrightarrow\; 0.$$

Thus $x_t \to z_*$ in the norm of $X$, for every choice of starting point $x_0 \in X$. $\blacksquare$

